    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im studying for my materials class and im a bit confused. why do we actually need to put steel rebar inside concrete for foundations? i get that concrete is strong, but whats the deal with the relationship between its compressive strength and the tensile strength of the steel? like why do they need each other?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Concrete is excellent at handling compressive forces but very weak in tension, meaning it cracks easily when pulled or bent. Steel rebar is added to carry those tensile loads, preventing cracks from spreading and holding the structure together. Together, they form a composite where concrete handles the squeezing and steel handles the pulling, creating a foundation that resists both forces effectively. This synergy is essential because without rebar, even a thick concrete slab would fail under the bending and stretching stresses of the ground shifting or heavy loads above.",
      "source": "target",
